# Build switches for speed, each at the value the board chose. On the
# PaperColor (story decode), the operator library at
# -O2 saved 1.74% a token, and the split at 64 columns gained nothing over 32;
# the per-token code in internal RAM saved 1.34% but needs 27 KB of internal
# SRAM, which the story spends on decode's activations instead (1.81%). An
# example project changes one in a defaults file of its own
# (examples/espressif/esp32s3: the sdkconfig.<switch>.defaults files and
# run_story_hardware.py --sdkconfig-extra), and builds it in a directory of its
# own.
menu "Espressif backend (ESP32-S3)"

config ESP32_BACKEND_OPS_O2
    bool "Compile the operator library at -O2 instead of -Os"
    default y
    help
        espressif_ops.cpp holds the custom ops and the portable fast paths
        a transformer decode runs between its delegate calls: the attention
        tail, the RMSNorms, RoPE, the KV-row writes, the scalar multiplies
        and the mask compares. -O2 inlines and unrolls them at the cost of
        code size: 6,448 B more flash in the story firmware, 14,128 B in the
        parity firmware, whose values do not change. The story decodes 1.74%
        faster (89.54 -> 87.99 ms/token on the PaperColor). The delegate's
        own -O2 is ESP32_BACKEND_O2, a CMake option.

config ESP32_BACKEND_SPLIT_MIN_COLS
    int "Narrowest projection the min-latency mode splits across both cores"
    default 32
    range 1 65536
    help
        Under the min_latency option a projection's output columns go half
        to each core, unless it has fewer columns than this: below it the
        fork/join handshake is expected to cost more than the columns it
        moves. The story Glimmer's K and V projections have 32 columns, so
        64 runs them on one core (16 of the 82 splits a token) and every
        wider projection still splits. A split moves whole columns, so the
        outputs are the same either way.

config ESP32_BACKEND_HOT_IRAM
    bool "Run the decode's per-token code from internal RAM"
    default n
    help
        Places the code a transformer decode runs on every token in internal
        RAM instead of flash: the functions marked ESP32_HOT_CODE (the
        delegate's dispatch and row loops, the activation quantiser, the
        split worker, the custom ops and fast paths between delegates), the
        PIE kernels and ExecuTorch's instruction loop (esp32_hot.lf). From
        flash that code competes for the 32 KB instruction cache, where each
        function lands depending on the size of unrelated code. The internal
        heap shrinks by what .iram0.text grows: 26,952 B in the story
        firmware, from the same RAM the KV cache, the delegate's internal
        scratch and the display allocate.

endmenu
